1 is available (unshown tiles have T1:2). But I already have a T2 and T3, so maybe T1 is not useful. Wait, I already have a T2 and two T3s. If I can get a T4, then T2-3-4. But I only have one T2. So if I discard T2, I lose the chance to form that chow. Alternatively, if I keep T2, maybe I can wait for T4.\n\nLooking at the F2 and J2, J3. Those are honor tiles. Maybe forming pungs or kongs? But I only have one of each. Unless I can get more. But given the unshown tiles, F2 has 3 left (since unshown F2:3?), wait the unshown tiles list says F2:3. So there are three F2s left. I have one F2. If I can get two more, that's a pung. But that's not likely soon.\n\nMy current hand is a mix of suited and honor tiles. The melds are all in characters. Maybe aiming for a half-flush (all in one suit plus honors) or something else.\n\nLet me check the scoring patterns. For example, if I can form more chows in the same suit, that could help. But I have a chow in characters (W1-2-3) and some tiles in bamboo and dots. Maybe it's better to focus on one suit.\n\nAlternatively, if I can form a pung or kong of honors. For example, if I can get another J2 or J3. But I have one J2 and one J3. The unshown J2 has 2 left, J3 has 3. So possible, but again not immediate.\n\nLooking at the valid actions, I need to choose which tile to discard. The options are:\n\n- J3: Discarding the white dragon. If I keep it, maybe I can form a pung later.\n- T2: Discarding the bamboo 2. If I keep it, maybe for a chow with T3 and T4.\n- B4: Discarding the dot 4. There are two B4s. If I discard one, maybe someone else can use it.\n- B2: Discarding the dot 2. Only one in hand.\n- T3: Discarding one of the two T3s. Since there are two, maybe keeping one is better.\n- F2: Discarding the wind 2. If I keep it, maybe for a pung.\n- J2: Discarding the green dragon. Similar to J3.\n- B5: Discarding the dot 5. If I have B4 and B5, maybe waiting for B6.\n- W4: Discarding the character 4. Since I have a chow of W1-2-3, W4 c